replies.body:
I chose close() deliberately because PipeliningClientExchangeHandlerImpl processes each response consumer in this order:

```
responseConsumer.responseCompleted(context);
T result = responseConsumer.getResult();
Exception ex = responseConsumer.getException();
responseConsumer.close();
```

responseCompleted() does not necessarily surface response-processing failures directly. For example, AbstractAsyncResponseConsumer.responseCompleted() catches exceptions thrown by buildResult() and exposes them later through getException(). The wrapper therefore captures that exception when Apache calls getException(), and then ends the per-request span from close().

We could call delegate.getException() directly after delegate.responseCompleted(), but that would still end the span too early for the close-failure path. A RuntimeException thrown by delegate.close() causes Apache to fail the aggregate Future. Since PipeliningRequestState.end() is idempotent, a span already ended in responseCompleted() could not be corrected to error afterward.

The response-processing and close-failure cases are covered by pipelinedFailureEndsAllStartedRequestSpansWithError() and pipelinedConsumerCloseFailureEndsSpanWithError(). I would prefer to keep close() as the end point so that the client span outcome remains consistent with the caller-visible Future outcome.

