replies.body:
Ending the span before delegate.close() would be simpler, but it would not be behaviorally equivalent.

delegate.close() can throw a RuntimeException. PipeliningClientExchangeHandlerImpl treats that as an execution failure, fails the aggregate Future, and propagates the exception. If state.end(exception) runs before delegate.close(), the span may already be ended successfully. Because PipeliningRequestState.end() is idempotent, the later failure callback cannot update that span with the close exception.

The RuntimeException catch is intentional:

```
catch (RuntimeException e) {
  state.end(e);
  throw e;
}
```

It keeps the client span consistent with the exceptionally completed Future, and pipelinedConsumerCloseFailureEndsSpanWithError() verifies this behavior.

The finally block also ensures that the span is ended when close() succeeds or throws an IOException. Apache catches and logs an IOException from responseConsumer.close() and continues processing the result, so that IOException should not replace the response-processing outcome recorded in exception.

For these reasons, I would keep state.end() after delegate.close() and retain the current catch/finally structure.

